WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:03.550
Forty marks, and the head teacher

2
00:00:03.550 --> 00:00:06.390
wants the class average, the top mark

3
00:00:06.390 --> 00:00:09.030
and how many failed, by noon.

4
00:00:09.770 --> 00:00:12.250
With forty separate variables

5
00:00:12.250 --> 00:00:14.970
I’d still be typing.

6
00:00:15.600 --> 00:00:19.480
A list holds all forty in one name.

7
00:00:20.680 --> 00:00:23.940
Today: lists, a tool for your marks.

8
00:00:25.280 --> 00:00:27.680
A list keeps many values, in

9
00:00:27.680 --> 00:00:29.360
order, under one name.

10
00:00:30.010 --> 00:00:31.130
Write them in square

11
00:00:31.130 --> 00:00:33.070
brackets, with commas between.

12
00:00:33.740 --> 00:00:36.000
Each value has a position, and

13
00:00:36.000 --> 00:00:37.500
positions count from zero.

14
00:00:38.200 --> 00:00:41.120
So marks at zero is the first mark.

15
00:00:42.390 --> 00:00:44.390
Python already knows the jobs

16
00:00:44.390 --> 00:00:45.670
teachers do with marks.

17
00:00:46.390 --> 00:00:47.370
len counts them.

18
00:00:47.970 --> 00:00:49.270
sum adds them up.

19
00:00:49.870 --> 00:00:52.570
max and min find the

20
00:00:52.570 --> 00:00:53.510
highest and the lowest.

21
00:00:54.320 --> 00:00:57.320
And sum divided by len is the average.

22
00:00:58.710 --> 00:00:59.910
To do something with each

23
00:00:59.910 --> 00:01:01.710
mark, loop over the list.

24
00:01:02.330 --> 00:01:05.010
for m in marks gives you

25
00:01:05.010 --> 00:01:06.230
each mark in turn.

26
00:01:06.840 --> 00:01:08.860
Here, the loop counts the pupils

27
00:01:08.860 --> 00:01:10.760
below ten, the pass mark.

28
00:01:11.380 --> 00:01:13.440
Counting is a running total

29
00:01:13.440 --> 00:01:15.300
that adds one each time.

30
00:01:16.520 --> 00:01:18.520
You can add to a list as marks

31
00:01:18.520 --> 00:01:19.840
come in, with append.

32
00:01:20.580 --> 00:01:23.420
Or keep the marks in a small file, one

33
00:01:23.420 --> 00:01:25.860
pupil per line, and let Python read it.

34
00:01:26.420 --> 00:01:29.080
A spreadsheet can save that file as CSV.

35
00:01:30.010 --> 00:01:31.310
Then the same program

36
00:01:31.310 --> 00:01:32.770
works for every class.

37
00:01:34.120 --> 00:01:34.520
Quick check!

38
00:01:35.140 --> 00:01:38.420
The list is fourteen, nine, seventeen.

39
00:01:39.350 --> 00:01:41.130
What does marks at one give?

40
00:01:42.400 --> 00:01:42.740
Nine.

41
00:01:43.470 --> 00:01:46.350
Position zero is the first mark, so

42
00:01:46.350 --> 00:01:48.130
position one is the second.

43
00:01:48.800 --> 00:01:50.960
And there’s no position three in a

44
00:01:50.960 --> 00:01:53.340
list of three: asking for it stops

45
00:01:53.340 --> 00:01:55.160
the program with an IndexError.

46
00:01:56.270 --> 00:01:57.890
With lists, a short

47
00:01:57.890 --> 00:01:59.550
program does real work.

48
00:02:00.210 --> 00:02:02.170
The class average and the top mark.

49
00:02:02.820 --> 00:02:05.380
How many pupils are below the pass mark.

50
00:02:06.060 --> 00:02:07.980
Marks sorted from highest to

51
00:02:07.980 --> 00:02:09.220
lowest with sorted.

52
00:02:09.900 --> 00:02:11.880
All on your phone, with no internet.

53
00:02:13.210 --> 00:02:14.190
A word on privacy.

54
00:02:15.070 --> 00:02:17.070
Practise with made-up marks,

55
00:02:17.310 --> 00:02:18.770
or first names only.

56
00:02:19.400 --> 00:02:21.420
A program on your phone keeps

57
00:02:21.420 --> 00:02:22.680
the data on your phone.

58
00:02:23.300 --> 00:02:26.000
Don’t paste a class list with full names

59
00:02:26.000 --> 00:02:27.820
into a website you don’t trust.

60
00:02:28.980 --> 00:02:31.260
In the app, open Sorting race.

61
00:02:32.020 --> 00:02:34.780
Python’s sorted does this work for you,

62
00:02:35.280 --> 00:02:37.400
but the race shows what happens inside.

63
00:02:38.120 --> 00:02:40.640
Race eight bars, then thirty-two,

64
00:02:40.900 --> 00:02:42.540
and watch the counts grow.

65
00:02:43.760 --> 00:02:44.500
Your turn.

66
00:02:45.140 --> 00:02:49.040
The marks are ten, twelve and fourteen.

67
00:02:49.860 --> 00:02:53.200
What does sum of marks divided

68
00:02:53.200 --> 00:02:55.860
by len of marks print?

69
00:02:57.230 --> 00:02:58.410
Twelve point zero.

70
00:02:59.100 --> 00:03:02.240
Remember: a list keeps many values under

71
00:03:02.240 --> 00:03:05.080
one name, and positions count from zero.

72
00:03:05.750 --> 00:03:10.130
len, sum, max and min do the common jobs.

73
00:03:10.850 --> 00:03:13.750
A loop over a list counts and totals.

74
00:03:14.350 --> 00:03:16.410
And practise with made-up data.

75
00:03:17.700 --> 00:03:20.380
Try it in class tomorrow: a class survey.

76
00:03:21.050 --> 00:03:23.110
Pupils vote for their favourite fruit,

77
00:03:23.610 --> 00:03:25.610
and you type the votes into a list.

78
00:03:26.210 --> 00:03:28.750
Let Python count each fruit with a

79
00:03:28.750 --> 00:03:30.730
loop, and show the class the result.
